"(ambitransitive, computing) To spawn a new child process by duplicating the existing process.", "tags": ["ambitransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "A parent process forks a child process, which in turn can fork other processes.", "reference": "2008, Mark G. Sobell, A Practical Guide to Fedora and Red Hat Enterprise Linux, Pearson Education, →ISBN:"}, {"text": "It appears that the shell forks a copy of itself and that this copy then forks to make each of the previous processes in the pipeline.", "reference": "2013, W. Richard Stevens, Stephen A. Rago, Advanced Programming in the UNIX Environment, 3rd edition, Addison-Wesley, →ISBN, page 304:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(ambitransitive, software engineering) To launch a separate software development effort based upon a modified copy of an existing software project, especially in free and open-source software.", "tags": ["ambitransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "For various reasons, McCool's server project subsequently forked, leading to the development of the Apache Web Server.", "reference": "2007, Fadi P. Deek, James A. M. McHugh, Open Source: Technology and Policy, Cambridge University Press, →ISBN, page 21:"}, {"text": "Google forked WebKit to create the Blink project in April 2013 because they wanted to make larger-scale changes to WebKit to fit their own needs that did not align well with the WebKit project itself.", "reference": "2015, Christian Bird et al., editors, The Art and Science of Analyzing Software Data, Elsevier, →ISBN, page 77:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, software engineering) To create a copy of a distributed version control repository.", "tags": ["ambitransitive", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "In this model, anyone can fork an existing repository and push changes to their personal fork.", "reference": "2015, Sajal